    "submission": "submission_c9.json",
    "summary": "Second obligation (action=obligation) plus a map extension, preflight-validated with valid=True and no diagnostics.\n\nSourced discrepancy: the appendEntries handler has two entry paths with different guards. processRPC applies checkRPCHeader (the protocol-version window gate, raft.go:43-71) before dispatching AppendEntries (raft.go:1390-1413), while processHeartbeat calls appendEntries directly with no such call (raft.go:1415-1436) and is installed as the transport heartbeat fast path at api.go:616; net_transport classifies a pure AppendEntries (Term != 0, leader address present, no entries, PrevLogEntry/PrevLogTerm zero, LeaderCommitIndex zero) as a heartbeat (net_transport.go:660-686) and hands it to that handler (net_transport.go:724-733). On that route appendEntries can still step the receiver down, persist a newer term, adopt the sender as known leader and refresh lastContact — so a peer outside the supported protocol window is rejected on one path and acted on by the other.\n\nProduct contents:\n- Obligation claim-heartbeat-header-gate (concern implementation_semantics): every AppendEntries RPC a server processes, including a pure heartbeat, is subject to the same RPC-header compatibility check as the normal path.\n- One question grounded on the existing principal Fact f-durable-term-vote (A2), direct Behavior b-heartbeat-fast-path (A1), lifecycle preservation, with supporting behaviors b-append-entries-sync (which locates the dropped guard and remaining effects) and b-term-authority (the A2 consequence).\n- One physical binding (symbol processHeartbeat, raft.go:1418-1436) with a single association over S40-S43, S16, S14.\n- Map extension (base version 4): new Behavior b-heartbeat-fast-path, the fast path recorded as a third producer of f-durable-term-vote (dependency change, meaning unchanged), and the matching Surface moved from deferred to mapped so the discrepancy stays visible in the behavior's guards and unknowns.\n\nRetained counterevidence and unknowns, not suppressed: the transport decodes the header before dispatch, so the version is known but unchecked; the fast path carries no log matching or commit propagation, confining any consequence to term/state/leader/lastContact; and the omission may be deliberate design assuming already-validated peers, in which case the correct disposition is a documented assumption rather than a defect. Reachability of an out-of-window peer and the election consequence are the explicit premises a later check must resolve; no violation is asserted yet."
